# RESEED (hand-harvest, local on fix/retirement, forward-only): a C-LEG CROSS-PLATFORM FIX — the raw-syscall
# helper preamble now emits an aarch64 branch (`svc #0`) instead of `#error` on non-x86_64. What this harvest
# carries:
#   - **§16 syscall aarch64 (compiler — codegen)**: since `src/runtime/arena.tks` (the §16 arena core) CALLS
#     `syscall6`/`syscall2` and is part of the corpus, the use-gate emits the `tk_syscallN` helpers into the
#     compiler's OWN `teko.c`. Those helpers were `#if defined(__x86_64__) … #else #error …#endif`, so EVERY
#     non-x86_64 C build (linux-arm64, macos-arm64) failed to compile (`#error`) — the cross-platform C-leg break
#     the owner observed ("one platform produced an artifact, the other didn't"). Fix (`codegen.tks`,
#     `cg_emit_syscall_helpers` + new `cg_emit_syscall_helper_text_aarch64`/`cg_emit_syscall_helpers_{x86_64,
#     aarch64}`): emit `#if defined(__x86_64__)||defined(__amd64__) … #elif defined(__aarch64__)||defined
#     (__arm64__) … #else #error`, where the aarch64 body uses the Linux aarch64 syscall ABI (nr in `x8`, args in
#     `x0..x5`, `svc #0`, return in `x0` via a `"+r"(x0)` in/out constraint, `"memory"` clobber). On macOS-arm64
#     the arena is UNWIRED so the helper is dead code — it only needs to COMPILE (`svc #0` is valid aarch64
#     assembly); it is not macOS-syscall-correct, which is native-lib terminal-phase territory. VERIFIED with a
#     real cross-compile: `clang -target aarch64-linux-gnu -c` of the emitted 7-arity helper block → a valid ARM
#     aarch64 ELF object, zero warnings.
#
# GATE: SELF-REPRODUCE — ONE-STEP LADDER (tc1 != tc2 == tc3).
#   - `cg_emit_syscall_helpers` decides the preamble a compiler emits FOR ITSELF from the compiler binary's OWN
#     compiled-in logic, not the source it is translating. So gen0 (the previous seed `26b6ca48`, old logic)
#     emits the OLD x86_64-only preamble at tc1 even while emitting the new helper-text bodies for later use; only
